 the liberty  loan parade and looked around to see who  was coughing and sneezing  the flu incubation period is typically  24 to 48 hours  and within a day or two of the parade  several hundred people  fell ill the hospitals began to fill  lines of desperate people formed waiting  to get in offering nurses bribes of a  hundred dollars or more to be admitted  crosby tells us visiting nurses often  walked into scenes resembling those of  the plague years of the 14th century  one nurse found a husband dead in the  same room where his wife  lay with newly born twins it had been 24  hours since the death and the births  and the wife had eaten no food but an  apple which happened to lie within reach  12 emergency hospitals would eventually  open to receive the growing number of  the sick  and dying like most big cities  philadelphia had a severe shortage of  doctors and nurses  850 of them were away on war duty  in philadelphia's general hospital 54  nurses ended up as patients  that's 43 percent of the entire nursing  staff  and 10 of them died  so the call went out for retired doctors  even medical students  all five philadelphia medical schools  closed and sent their third and fourth  year students to help  on october 3rd croissant finally ordered  all schools and churches and theaters to  close and band all public gatherings  most grocers were closed and few stores  of any kind were open  but the closing of the schools and the  churches in particular  further isolated citizens from one  another and contributed  to a growing climate of fear an  editorial in the philadelphia inquirer  asks  since crowds gather in congested eating  places and press into elevators and hang  to the straps of ill-ventilated street  cars  it's a little difficult to understand  what is to be gained by  shutting up well-ventilated churches and  theaters  the authorities seem to be going daft  what are they trying to do  scare everybody to death  shutting down bars and taverns created  some  unusual problems because now  you could only get whiskey at the  drugstore and only with a prescription  so drugstores became crowded with long  lines of people  buying whiskey a few unscrupulous  pharmacists pushed the price of whiskey  as high as  52 dollars a gallon a princely sum  in 1918. so many people took the ferry  to nearby camden because the bars there  were still  open the city of camden had to shut down  all of its taverns to stem the tide of  new customers  who were bearing the flu  and in retrospect the closings were  probably unnecessary  for cities like philadelphia that  shuttered public places the case rate  and the death rate were no better than  for cities that left them open  as the true horror of the flu took hold  philadelphia became a city of the dead  isaac starr a university of pennsylvania  student wrote that the life of the city  had almost stopped absentee rates ran  from 20 to 40 percent  for those businesses and factories that  still remained open  the local press and public health  officials continued to lie to the public  about the epidemic insisting that the  worst was over  and they created a prevailing attitude  of public mistrust of government at  every level in early october croissant  insisted that quote  these deaths mark the high water mark in  the fatalities and it's fair to assume  that from this time until the epidemic  is crushed  the death rate will consistently be  lowered  the next day 428 people  died and the body count kept going  higher  and higher  citizens took the hanging crepe paper on  their doors to show that someone had  died  white paper meant that a child or young  adult had died  black meant that the victim was  middle-aged and gray was used to mark  the death of the elderly  the suburb of west manila a mob of  weeping housewives actually blocked the  car of a local doctor who was making a  house call  and would not let him leave until he had  treated every child in the neighborhood  all 57 of them  so many phone workers were out sick that  calls were limited to emergencies only  bell telephone took out ads in the local  papers to announce that no calls would  be accepted  quote other than absolutely necessary  calls compelled by the epidemic  or by war necessity one such ad  reads telephone service faces a crisis  the situation is one which the public  must meet squarely  800 operators 27 percent of our force  are now absent due to influenza it is  every person's duty to the community to  cut out every call that is not  absolutely necessary that the essential  needs of the government  doctors and nurses may be cared for  crosby tells us that dr croyson  empowered the phone company to cut  service to any customers that made  unnecessary calls  and that about 1 000 people were  actually disconnected for violating that  rule  as the casualties continued to mount  orphans and starving children became a  problem  there was no one left alive to feed them  as the epidemic peaked during the week  of october 16  4597  died 759 of them in a single day october  the 10th  with a severe shortage of undertakers  and gravediggers the bodies piled up  rapidly  prisoners seminary students and  clergymen were pressed into service  and to bury its poorest citizens the  city finally resorted to trench graves  dug by steam shovels in potter's field  some undertakers took to profiteering  raising rates up to  600 percent and some cemetery workers  and gravediggers demanded an extra fee  of 15  or more and even insisted after being  bribed that the customers dig their  family's graves themselves  citizens were left with wooden boxes and  instructed to leave their dead on the  front porch  in some cases the bodies were simply  tossed in a heap on wagons  reminiscent of the play gears in  medieval europe  many people were forced to live with the  dead often for  several days closing them off in a  separate room if they had the luxury  or just wrapping them in a sheet and  leaving them in a corner  citizens became increasingly isolated  huddled behind closed doors  ambulances were supplemented with  private cars police cars  anything with wheels taxi drivers were  pressed into service and to their  infinite credit not a  single taxi driver in philadelphia  refused to carry the sick and the dying  to the hospital  every social agency in the city public  and private  chipped in to help as best they could  without regard to race  or creed or color idol  teachers volunteered in droves along  with nuns and priests  boy scouts firemen policemen and  hundreds and hundreds of private  citizens  archbishop dennis dougherty sent 1 000  sisters of saint joseph out into the  community  to help care for the sick by october the  18th  the worst was over and the emergency  hospitals began to close  on sunday the 27th churches were  reopened and school started the next day  and on the 30th the bars and the  theaters reopened  followed by a noticeable spike in  arrests for drunk and disorderly conduct  as many as 500 000 citizens had been  infected and at least  12 897 had died  and those numbers are very conservative  as thousands of cases went unreported in  the chaos  that had descended on the city and  all across america and the aftermath of  the flu  the fate of the survivors became a major  problem  many families were impoverished with one  or more breadwinners sick  or dead and the flu left behind a world  filled with widows and orphans 21 000  orphans from the flu  in new york city alone  deaths of 15 to 34 year olds rose to 20  times the normal rate in a flu epidemic  so it wasn't just the very young  or the very old who died this time  why did so many more young adults die  we're going to try to answer that  question in our next lecture  more elderly people survive than usual  we think because they might have been  exposed to a similar virus earlier in  their lives  and that initial attack may have been so  mild it wasn't noticed but it left them  with at leas