 other hand  survived without a single victim because  of its  early and very effective quarantine  australia had the lowest global death  rate also thanks to an early quarantine  at the very end a troop ship with 96  soldiers did finally sneak into port but  by then the virus had weakened  in chiapas mexico 10 of the population  died  in the fiji islands 14 percent of the  population died  in 16 days  in noam alaska out of 300 inuits 176  died and many inuit villages were  completely wiped out  with some villages sustaining 85 percent  casualties  or more over 20 million people are now  thought to have died in india  alone one delhi hospital treated 13  000 flu patients only to watch over  seven thousand of them  die an estimated one-third of the entire  population of labrador died  ococ labrador 266 citizens  only 59 survivors hebron 150 inuits dead  out of 220 and in many rural and  isolated areas packs of wild dogs became  a serious problem  starving dogs often broke into dwellings  to feast on the dead or  on those too sick to fight back the rev  andrew asbo shot over 100 dogs  before he was rescued  the virus killed an estimated 7 percent  of the entire population of russia and  iran  these kinds of epidemics are sometimes  called virgin  soil epidemics everyone on earth  at one time was probably exposed to that  virus  those who survived the infection formed  an immune reaction so in the end  the virus had no place left to go it  couldn't maintain itself  in the human population  well fortunately for the flu virus at  any rate it doesn't need humanity to  sustain itself  birds are its primary host and as long  as the bird populations are healthy  the flu will always find a home  at the height of the second wave victor  vaughn nearly lost  hope he writes if the epidemic continues  its mathematical rate of acceleration  civilization could easily disappear from  the face of the  earth within a matter of a few more  weeks  but by late november of 1918 the crest  of the influenza deaths had passed it  was the worst  epidemic in the history of mankind  many great cities like philadelphia  ground to a halt for several weeks  during the pandemic  and as the city of brotherly love  reminds us these disasters bring out  both the best  and the worst in humanity  so what can we do if this killer virus  returns  in our next lecture we're going to wrap  ourselves in the cloak of sherlock  holmes  we're going to follow the trail of one  of the most curious detective stories of  modern times  the search to recover an intact flu  virus  a discovery that could help prevent the  next pandemic  we're also going to learn how the 1918  flu may have helped to set the stage for  world war  ii by infecting president wilson at a  critical stage  of the peace talks

