was so bad at that time  and so corrupt  that lincoln stefans called it the worst  governed city in america  and its machine appointed health  director dr wilner crewson  was honest but inexperienced over  cautious  and in the end completely ineffective  despite frequent warnings despite  mounting military cases  he made no plans for an epidemic he made  no stockpiles of medicine or equipment  he didn't even draw up a list of first  responders of doctors and nurses to  contact  in an emergency the news of the epidemic  raging in boston arrived in philadelphia  too late to put the city on alert and  besides the ama journal had announced in  september that the flu was nothing  special and had quote  already practically disappeared from the  allied troops  september 28 1918 oh life in philly was  great  the biggest local news story was the  ongoing coal miner strike and the  upcoming parade  the liberty lone parade designed to sell  warbonds was to be the biggest parade  in the city's history flu had already  arrived in the city hitting the navy  yard in mid-september  and the day before the parade over 200  fluke cases were reported  123 of them civilian  navy personnel tried to stop the parade  but with no success  morale trumped public health  dr crewson continued to insist there was  no danger of an epidemic  it was only as he called it  old-fashioned influenza or grip  that enormous crowd of several hundred  thousand  were packed together for hours as that  two-mile long parade flowed by  airplanes flew overhead anti-aircraft  guns fired live  shells rigged to explode at a low  altitude  now i had to miss mardi gras parades  this year to record these lectures but i  managed to squeeze in a few parades  before i left  and as i was standing on st charles  avenue watching muses flow down the  street  watching float after float after float i  couldn't think about  all the people with their hands in the  air saying throw me something mister  all i could think about was the liberty  loan parade and looked around to see who  was coughing and sneezing  the flu incubation period is typically  24 to 48 hours  and within a day or two of the parade  several hundred people  fell ill the hospitals began to fill  lines of desperate people formed waiting  to get in offering nurses bribes of a  hundred dollars or more to be admitted  crosby tells us visiting nurses often  walked into scenes resembling those of  the plague years of the 14th century  one nurse found a husband dead in the  same room where his wife  lay with newly born twins it had been 24  hours since the death and the births  and the wife had eaten no food but an  apple which happened to lie within reach  12 emergency hospitals would eventually  open to receive the growing number of  the sick  and dying like most big cities  philadelphia had a severe shortage of  doctors and nurses  850 of them were away on war duty  in philadelphia's general hospital 54  nurses ended up as patients  that's 43 percent of the entire nursing  staff  and 10 of them died  so the call went out for retired doctors  even medical students  all five philadelphia medical schools  closed and sent their third and fourth  year students to help  on october 3rd croissant finally ordered  all schools and churches and theaters to  close and band all public gatherings  most grocers were closed and few stores  of any kind were open  but the closing of the schools and the  churches in particular  further isolated citizens from one  another and contributed  to a growing climate of fear an  editorial in the philadelphia inquirer  asks  since crowds gather in congested eating  places and press into elevators and hang  to the straps of ill-ventilated street  cars  it's a little difficult to understand  what is to be gained by  shutting up well-ventilated churches and  theaters  the authorities seem to be going daft  what are they trying to do  scare everybody to death  shutting down bars and taverns created  some  unusual problems because now  you could only get whiskey at the  drugstore and only with a prescription  so drugstores became crowded with long  lines of people  buying whiskey a few unscrupulous  pharmacists pushed the price of whiskey  as high as  52 dollars a gallon a princely sum  in 1918. so many people took the ferry  to nearby camden because the bars there  were still  open the city of camden had to shut down  all of its taverns to stem the tide of  new customers  who were bearing the flu  and in retrospect the closings were  probably unnecessary  for cities like philadelphia that  shuttered public places the case rate  and the death rate were no better than  for cities that left them open  as the true horror of the flu took hold  philadelphia became a city of the dead  isaac starr a university of pennsylvania  student wrote that the life of the city  had almost stopped absentee rates ran  from 20 to 40 percent  for those businesses and factories that  still remained open  the local press and public health  officials continued to lie to the public  about the epidemic insisting that the  worst was over  and they created a prevailing attitude  of public mistrust of government at  every level in early october croissant  insisted that quote  these deaths mark the high water mark in  the fatalities and it's fair to assume  that from this time until the epidemic  is crushed  the death rate will consistently be  lowered  the next day 428 people  died and the body count kept going  higher  and higher  citizens took the hanging crepe paper on  their doors to show that someone had  died  white paper meant that a child or young  adult had died  black meant that the victim was  middle-aged and gray was used to mark  the death of the elderly  the suburb of west manila a mob of  weeping housewives actually blocked the  car of a local doctor who was making a  house call  and would not let him leave until he had  treated every child in the neighborhood  all 57 of them  so many phone workers were out sick that  calls were limited to emergencies only  bell telephone took out ads in the local  papers to announce that no calls would  be accepted  quote other than absolutely necessary  calls compelled by the epidemic  or by war necessity one such ad  reads telephone service faces a crisis  the situation is one which the public  must meet squarely  800 operators 27 percent of our force  are now absent due to influenza it is  every person's duty to the community to  cut out every call that is not  absolutely necessary that the essential  needs of the government  doctors and nurses may be cared for  crosby tells us that dr croyson  empowered the phone company to cut  service to any customers that made  unnecessary calls  and that about 1 000 people were  actually disconnected for violating that  rule  as the casualties continued to mount  orphans and starving children became a  problem  there was no one left alive to feed them  as the epidemic peaked during the week  of october 16  4597  died 759 of them in a single day october  the 10th  with a severe shortage of undertakers  and gravediggers the bodies piled up  rapidly  prisoners seminary students and  clergymen were pressed into service  and to bury its poorest citizens the  city finally resorted to trench graves  dug by steam shovels in potter's field  some undertakers took to profiteering  raising rates up to  600 percent and some cemetery workers  and gravediggers demanded an extra fee  of 15  or more and even insisted after being  bribed that the customers dig their  family's graves themselves  citizens were left with wooden boxes and  instructed to leave their dead on the  front porch  in some cases the bodies were simply  tossed in a heap on wagons  reminiscent of the play gears in  medieval europe  many people were forced to live with the  dead often for  several days closing them off in a  separate room if they had the luxury  or just wrapping them in a sheet and  leaving them in a corner  citizens became increasingly isolated  huddled behind closed doors  ambulances were supplemented with  private cars police cars  anything with wheels taxi drivers were  pressed into service and to their  infinite credit not a  single taxi driver in philadelphia  refused to carry the sick and the dying  to the hospital  every social agency in the city public  and private  chipped in to help as best they could  without regard to race  or creed or color idol  teachers volunteered in droves along  with nuns and priests  boy scouts firemen policemen and  hundreds and hundreds of private  citizens  archbishop dennis dougherty sent 1 000  sisters of saint joseph out into the  community  to help care for the sick by october the  18th  the worst was over and the emergency  hospitals began to close  on sunday the 27th churches were  reopened and school started the next day  and on the 30th the bars and the  theaters reopened  followed by a noticeable spike in  arrests for drunk and disorderly conduct  as many as 500 000 citizens had been  infected and at least  12 897 had died  and those numbers are very conservative  as thousands of cases went unreported in  the chaos  that had descended on the city and  all across america and the aftermath of  the flu  the fate of the survivors became a major  problem  many families were impoverished with one  or more breadwinners sick  or dead and the flu left behind a world  filled with widows and orphans 21 000  orphans from the flu  in new york city alone  deaths of 15 to 34 year olds rose to 20  times the normal rate in a flu epidemic  so it wasn't just the very young  or the very old who died this time  why did so many more young adults die  we're going to try to answer that  question in our next lecture  more elderly people survive than usual  we think because they might have been  exposed to a similar virus earlier in  their lives  and that initial attack may have been so  mild it wasn't noticed but it left them  with at least  partial immunity to the new related  virus  influenza is a little unusual in that  new strains are  fiercely competitive with one another  and this is an interesting example of  competitive exclusion which we learned  about in our very first lecture  one competitor is so successful it  drives the other  into local extinction the flu  triggers the body's immune response  against any and all strains of the flu  that that person has ever come in  contact with  so an existing strain that tries to  reinfect someone will find the door  already closed and will rapidly die out  as a result  so only one subtype of flu can exist in  the human population  at one time  now as time goes on fewer and fewer  people are creating antibodies for other  forms of the flu so a fresh drain  now has an open playing field competing  strains  usually coexist for a brief period a few  months perhaps  and the only way to trace the path of  the flu is by serological archaeology  what a fascinating field taking blood  samples from various age groups  and examining the antibodies to see what  strains each age group had been exposed  to  and try to link those up with recorded  instances of epidemics or pandemics  the third and final wave followed in  late 1918 and  into early 1919 and the flu at this  point  seems to have gradually mutated into a  weaker strain because the third way was  short it was sharp  but relatively mild compared to that  killer second wave  the final toll was grim estimates of  american dead run to 675  000 and that's out of a population of  only 105  million britain lost 228  000 to the flu the best global estimate  out of 1.8 billion  is 50 to 100 million dead and that comes  from nobel laureate and flu expert dr  mcfarland burnett  naive native populations were especially  hard hit  many isolated populations avoid regular  contact with common viruses  and that leaves them highly vulnerable  in pandemics because they have no  immunity whatsoever  american samoa on the other hand  survived without a single victim because  of its  early and very effective quarantine  australia had the lowest global death  rate also thanks to an early quarantine  at the very end a troop ship with 96  soldiers did finally sneak into port but  by then the virus had weakened  in chiapas mexico 10 of the population  died  in the fiji islands 14 percent of the  population died  in 16 days  in noam alaska out of 300 inuits 176  died and many inuit villages were  completely wiped out  with some villages sustaining 85 percent  casualties  or more over 20 million people are now  thought to have died in india  alone one delhi hospital treated 13  000 flu patients only to watch over  seven thousand of them  die an estimated one-third of the entire  population of labrador died  ococ labrador 266 citizens  only 59 survivors hebron 150 inuits dead  out of 220 and in many rural and  isolated areas packs of wild dogs became  a serious problem  starving dogs often broke into dwellings  to feast on the dead or  on those too sick to fight back the rev  andrew asbo shot over 100 dogs  before he was rescued  the virus killed an estimated 7 percent  of the entire population of russia and  iran  these kinds of epidemics are sometimes  called virgin  soil epidemics everyone on earth  at one time was probably exposed to that  virus  those who survived the infection formed  an immune reaction so in the end  the virus had no place left to go it  couldn't maintain itself  in the human population  well fortunately for the flu virus at  any rate it doesn't need humanity to  sustain itself  birds are its primary host and as long  as the bird populations are healthy  the flu will always find a home  at the height of the second wave victor  vaughn nearly lost  hope he writes if the epidemic continues  its mathematical rate of acceleration  civilization could easily disappear from  the face of the  earth within a matter of a few more  weeks  but by late november of 1918 the crest  of the influenza deaths had passed it  was the worst  epidemic in the history of mankind  many great cities like philadelphia  ground to a halt for several weeks  during the pandemic  and as the city of brotherly love  reminds us these disasters bring out  both the best  and the worst in humanity  so what can we do if this killer virus  returns  in our next lecture we're going to wrap  ourselves in the cloak of sherlock  holmes  we're going to follow the trail of one  of the most curious detective stories of  modern times  the search to recover an intact flu  virus  a discovery that could help prevent the  next pandemic  we're also going to learn how the 1918  flu may have helped to set the stage for  world war  ii by infecting president wilson at a  critical stage  of the peace talks
 

